Filetype: IR signals file
Version: 1
#
# ==============================================================
#  Meeting_Room_Deflector.ir
#
#  >>> PRIVACY-MODE IS STICKY ON PURPOSE. <<<
#  If you load and broadcast these codes in a meeting you intended
#  to stay visible in, you WILL Display_Off the room's projector.
#  Treat this palette as a one-way shield: only load right before a
#  presentation where you genuinely want the room's A/V muted.
#
#  Defensive-use IR signal palette. Loads onto the Flipper under
#  Infrared -> Saved, then "Send" broadcasts these NEC codes
#  against a chosen TV / projector / soundbar within range
#  (typically 5-10 m line-of-sight).
#
#  Twist vs. a vanilla remote capture: this is a defensive
#  "engage privacy mode" palette -- Mute + Display_Off for
#  common enterprise A/V gear, intended to silence a screen
#  if BugReaperX / Vanthryx output accidentally gets cast to a
#  shared projector.
#
#  The address/command values are SYNTHESIZED, NOT captured from
#  any real device, so they will not match your specific A/V
#  remote. They are structurally VALID NEC codes though (see
#  below), so the file parses, transmits and works as a generic
#  NEC remote -- but to actually control your hardware, learn the
#  real codes: Infrared -> Learn, then replace the fields.
#
#  NEC protocol notes:
#    - address: and command: are stored as 4-byte little-endian
#      raw values (e.g. command 07 00 00 00 = NEC command byte
#      0x07). The Flipper encoder automatically appends each
#      byte's complement on the wire (0x07 -> 07 F8), so you
#      never write the complement yourself.
#    - 8-bit address 0x04, commands 0x07 (Mute) / 0x08
#      (Display_Off) / 0x15 (Volume_Down x3).
# ==============================================================
#
name: Mute
type: parsed
protocol: NEC
address: 04 00 00 00
command: 07 00 00 00
#
name: Display_Off
type: parsed
protocol: NEC
address: 04 00 00 00
command: 08 00 00 00
#
name: Volume_Down_x3
type: parsed
protocol: NEC
address: 04 00 00 00
command: 15 00 00 00
#
# Notes for the operator:
#   - Use Infrared -> Saved -> [Hold signal to edit] to
#     rename or re-order; the Flipper reads these per
#     record, not as a single script.
#   - To add a new code, append a `name:` / `type:` /
#     `protocol:` / `address:` / `command:` block separated
#     by a `#` blank line.
#   - To make these control YOUR A/V gear, learn the real
#     remote's codes first and paste them in place of the
#     synthesized values above.
#
# Validate with:  python ..\scripts\verify_flipper_files.py
# Data tests:     python ..\scripts\test_flipper_payloads.py
